findings.fix:
In syntacticAnalysis, skip '@' while pushing instead of testing the whole string:

    analysis_s.pop();
    string right = rule_temp.right[0];
    for (int i = (int)right.length() - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
        if (right[i] != '@') analysis_s.push(right[i]);
    }

This makes the push loop agree with firstOf()'s treatment of '@' and keeps the existing `X::=@` behaviour unchanged (the whole right part is skipped, nothing is pushed).

Either reject '#' in the raw input right after `cin >> input` (`if (input.find('#') != string::npos) { cout << "输入串中不能包含结束标志“#”" << endl; success = false; }`), or tighten the accept test to require the input stack to be exhausted:

    if (top == '#' && cur == '#' && input_s.size() == 1) { ... }

and let the existing terminal-mismatch branch reject the leftover.

The guard is meant to catch non-termination (left recursion), which shows up as expansions that consume no input, not as a large absolute step count. Either scale the budget with the input, e.g.

    unsigned int limit = 1000 + 20 * (unsigned int)(input.length() + 1);
    if (++step > limit) { ... }

or better, count only consecutive non-consuming expansions and reset the counter on every successful terminal match, tripping after e.g. table.row.size()+1 expansions without consuming input.

Detect the collision before overwriting and warn:

    unsigned int at = r * table.col.size() + c;
    if (!table.rules[at].right.empty() && !(table.rules[at] == cell)) {
        cout << "文法不是LL(1)文法：(" << rules[i].left << "," << *it
             << ")处存在冲突，产生式 " << table.rules[at] << " 与 " << cell << " 相斥" << endl;
    }
    table.rules[at] = cell;

(comparing right[0] strings is enough; no operator== needed).

Give firstOf a non-terminal test consistent with the rest of the program — either pass the non_terminal set down, or extend the check:

    static bool is_non_terminal(const char c, const vector<rule>& rules) {
        for (unsigned int i = 0; i < rules.size(); i++)
            if (rules[i].left == c) return true;
        return isupper((unsigned char)c) != 0;   // 只出现在右部的大写字母同样是非终结符
    }

With that, a non-terminal with no production contributes nothing to FIRST (the alternative loop finds no rules, has_epsilon stays false, all_epsilon is cleared) and First(Xa) prints as {}.
